<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Life of Lycurgus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Life of Lycurgus is a biography of the legendary Spartan lawgiver by the Greek writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, part of his Parallel Lives, where it is paired with the life of the Roman king Numa. It describes the Spartan constitution, the redistribution of land, the common meals and the education of boys, and preserves the text of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-rhetra">Great Rhetra</a>. It shaped the image of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> for later writers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Machiavelli</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/life-of-lycurgus.jpg" alt="Life of Lycurgus" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Plutarch · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plutarch,_Parallel_Lives,_Oxford,_MS._Canonici_Greek_93_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
